<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Tree Grows in Brooklyn (Betty Smith)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Premature Burial (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1962</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody played in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Daughter of Molly Malone (Bing Crosby and Rosemary Clooney)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">updated version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Clockwork Orange (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Molly Malone (Dublin Millennium charity single)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1988</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fair City (Irish soap opera)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the opening line</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My Son, the Celebrity (Allan Sherman)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody in a medley</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sweet Molly Malone (Apollo's Medley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1790</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier song with a heroine of the same name</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Widow Malone</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1809</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier song whose heroine is also called Molly Malone</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pat Corney's Account of Himself</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1826</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier song with a similar opening and closing</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Meet Me Miss Molly Malone</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1836</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier song with a heroine of the same name</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cockles and Mussels (J. B. Geoghegan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1876</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a London song with a very similar chorus</span></li></ul></section>
